{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head f760e3714b536c7380983cdb882c8e3f22b83445 on planner/act-environment-20261114, its one artifact `notes/2026-11-14-act-environment.md`, for request 30400d34 under implementation promise 02296e26 and my promise 80204015. The note designs Explore as the second half of the one judge, and it keeps every new signature behind a retained authorization. Each dap fact that it cites is true at main 189cf7b6.\n\nWhat I checked, item by item:\n\n1. Cited facts at main 189cf7b6.\n   - The three partial copies of admission: `actsOn` filters by one guard form (`forms.ts:45`). `docs/application-views.md` line 195 has the quoted host sentence.\n   - `judge.ts`: `JudgeContext` is lines 43 to 92. `judgesTime` is line 114. `Refused` with `name` is line 148. `judgeAct` calls `actJudged` at 184, and `actJudged` verifies the signature at 219. `view.accepted(actor, key)` is at 228. Lines 295 and 305 add `on` and each `also` name to the compared list.\n   - The ID of an opened item is the entry's sequence: `effects.ts:330` writes `item: j.self`, and the judge sets `self` to `scope.head.seq + 1` (`judge.ts:326`). A new item has revision 1 (`item-effects.ts:36`).\n   - `shapeDeclaredAct` is `declared.ts:198`, and `AskedOf` is `declared.ts:67`. `REFUSAL_CLASSES` is `records.ts:91`. `Intent.actor` is `contract/src/intent.ts:42`.\n   - `validate/shape.ts:64` refuses a key outside the two lists. `validate/handlers.ts:193` calls it for each act.\n   - `device-command.ts:55`, `gitread.ts:249`, `hold.ts:258` and `genesis.ts:152` require an empty `expected`.\n   - `room.json` and `site.json` count 22 and 2 lines with `\"from\": \"self\"`.\n   - The note's claim that an absent optional field applies no effect matches DG12 of the contract note.\n   - The D12 citations hold: rules 3 and 5 of section 4, the bounds of 8 envelopes and 64 signings (5.1), `resign` (5.2), `absent` (5.4 and 5.5), the `busy` stop at step 3 of `submit` (section 8), no export to `contract` or `derive` (section 10), the room page's copies (11), seven invariants (12), and the choices of sections 14 and 15.\n   - The kernel citations hold: the test of section 2, promises 3 and 9, K5, the review rule of section 7 with rulings `08c5ff25` and `4b5e0ec2`, the hold of 7.1, the format binding of 7.2, the Rules 14 cohort of 7.3, and the transport agreement of 7.4.\n2. Section 16 gives each of its three parts a place, a reason under section 7 and a decision. The export of E1 adds to the kernel and closes no K item, so under section 7.1 it waits for the ruling that the note says the planner files. Decision A waits for its own design and its ruling. The `ScopeApi` method is held, and no step builds it.\n3. Section 13 gives each step a need that exists: E1 needs the export ruling. E2 needs C2. E3 needs C2 and C4. E4 needs C1, which landed, and C3. E5 needs E4. E6 needs E3 to E5. Step A comes last. Section 15 pairs E3 with C4 and E4 with C3, so no build holds the old rule 5.\n4. The two facts that the note left open:\n   - The hosted MCP server does not read scope state directly. `HostedAgent` runs in its own Durable Object (`packages/scope/src/worker-mcp.ts`). It reaches a scope through the `fetch` port, which calls `route()` over the native routes (line 353), and through the `observe` and `roleStanding` ports. It holds no state of the scope it acts on. So E2 needs a replica in the agent host, and its dependency on C2 stands.\n   - A new definition part probably needs an install gate. The precedent is the `reference` field: it stays in `artroom-definition-1`, and `requireReferenceInstall` (`packages/platform/src/application-install-reference.ts`) refuses it under any Rules pin except the Commons cohort. The comment there says each native Rules pin opts in explicitly. An optional part that a newer validator accepts would need the same opt-in, unless it comes in a new format. I did not read how `level` and `capacity` install. The design of step A must state its gate.\n\nCheap checks: the head changes one path against main 189cf7b6, and main is an ancestor. The commit-message check passed at the head (3 checked, 0 flagged), as the request states. Under f45b355a a notes-only head runs no test, and I ran none.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One body of code judges an intent body, for the commit and for Explore, so the three partial copies of admission can go. The bound on the replay corpus is the right condition for the split. A retained authorization with fixed versions replaces \"no timer signs\" with a predicate that the suite can check, and the default deadline keeps the behavior that a member sees. The order E1 first and decision A last lets each step land with no kernel change except the one reviewed export.\n\nOne precision for the build of step A: lines 295 and 305 only add a subject to the compared list. The one comparison is line 312, and line 309 requires one key in `expected` for each subject that the list holds. Under `current`, step A changes the list, and both checks follow from it. Today only a `transition` compares the revision of `on`. A `comment` on an item compares none, so decision A changes nothing for a comment's `on`.\n\nSecurity: no finding. To construct, move or explore an act gives no authority. Explore signs nothing and sends nothing, and it answers `unavailable` instead of inventing a grant, a fact or the intent digest. The descriptor digest is fixed at the first submit, so a later package cannot widen a retained step. Only the owner key signs for a step, and a second device signs nothing under its key. The rule against an `observed` name bound through a slot of a `current` subject closes the case where a reused revision number would hide a change of item.\n\nSimplification: no required change. The coarse rule for `{ entry }` and the recomputation at each view and tick keep the first version free of a read record. Section 5.6 states that its cost is not measured, and E3 should measure it.\n\nKernel: the note adds one export to `packages/derive` in E1, for the reason of promise 9, and it closes no K item. It waits for the planner's ruling under sections 7 and 7.1. Decision A adds one form to the K5 count and needs a carrier under section 7.2. The note adds no scope kind, platform name, capability, tool family or record model.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing, ruling or build. Hugh's ratification and the ruling for the export of E1 stay with the planner. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1c68a5afa97e5647cad53d4fecd4d110ea1aa26d","binding":"assigned","head":"f760e3714b536c7380983cdb882c8e3f22b83445","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:80204015d15ab1e5678906fdd0d3ba350668f3b5\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:02296e26fc47c4839f910ac309ded9c03d9870a9\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:80204015d15ab1e5678906fdd0d3ba350668f3b5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}